## 6. Go to Definition

Suppose you have:

```ts
function calculateTotal(price: number, quantity: number) {
    return price * quantity;
}

const total = calculateTotal(10, 3);
```

Put the cursor on:

```text
calculateTotal
```

and press:

```text
F12
```

VS Code jumps to:

```ts
function calculateTotal(...)
```

This is extremely useful in TypeScript projects.

---

# 7. Find References

Put the cursor on:

```ts
calculateTotal
```

and press:

```text
Shift + F12
```

VS Code shows where that function is used.

This is especially useful in large TypeScript projects.

Conceptually:

```text
function
   ↓
Find References
   ↓
┌──────────────┐
│ file1.ts     │
│ file5.ts     │
│ component.ts │
│ service.ts   │
└──────────────┘
```

---

# 8. Rename Symbol

Suppose you have:

```ts
const userName = "Mike";

console.log(userName);
```

Put your cursor on `userName`:

```text
F2
```

Change it to:

```text
name
```

VS Code can update the references:

```ts
const name = "Mike";

console.log(name);
```

This is much better than doing a blind text replacement.

---

# 9. Quick Fix

One of the most useful shortcuts:

```text
Ctrl + .
```

Example:

```ts
const user = {
    name: "Mike"
};

console.log(username);
```

If TypeScript detects a problem, `Ctrl + .` can show possible fixes.

Depending on the situation, it may offer things such as:

```text
Add missing import
Add type annotation
Convert to ...
Remove unused ...
```

Think of:

```text
Ctrl + .
```

as:

> **"VS Code, how can I fix this?"**

---

# 10. Auto import

TypeScript projects benefit greatly from automatic imports.

Suppose you have:

```ts
import { UserService } from "./services/UserService";
```

Instead of manually typing the import, you can sometimes type:

```ts
const service = new UserService();
```

and VS Code offers:

```text
Add import from "./services/UserService"
```

Press:

```text
Enter
```

and it inserts the import.

